/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"production-level-4","number":4,"title":"Module 4 — Session Labs: Pop and R&B","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Skip one, many, or all. Pop default remains. Gate C (Module 7) does not require a lab. If you skip, Deliverable 1 uses Module 0 pop form + Module 2 Session craft. Do not invent BPMs. Do not open Operator. Do not treat a lab sketch as Delive","modules":[{"id":"production-m-4-1","code":"4","title":"Modules 3–6 opener — Session genre labs","blurb":"Skip one, many, or all. Pop default remains. Gate C (Module 7) does not require a lab. If you skip, Deliverable 1 uses Module 0 pop form + Module 2 Session craft. Do not invent BPMs. Do not open Operator. Do not treat a lab sketch as Delive","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-7","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Modules 3–6 opener — Session genre labs","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>All sections in Modules 3–6 are skippable.</strong> Same seven-step pedagogy every time: listen and label → tempo only if cited → scene map → chops the genre actually uses (production, not sound design) → contrast as form → MK4 launch → 32–64 bar sketch + written list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Skip one, many, or all. <strong>Pop default remains.</strong> Gate C (Module 7) does not require a lab. If you skip, Deliverable 1 uses Module 0 pop form + Module 2 Session craft."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent BPMs. Do not open Operator. Do not treat a lab sketch as Deliverable 1 unless you later <em>choose</em> that genre in 7.1."},{"type":"heading","text":"Module 4 — Pop and R&B labs (skippable)","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Apply the Module 0 form textbook on the Session grid you built in Module 2.   <strong>Do not:</strong> write a hit-lyric unit (Berklee lyric courses exist). Do not design a new synth to make a chorus."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 4.1 — G-POP · Pop / radio (skippable)","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Map ABABCB (or verse–pre–chorus) onto scenes. Chorus clips higher-energy than verse clips <strong>without</strong> new synthesis.   <strong>Applies:</strong> Sections 0.4–0.8. Wikipedia modern-pop string + KB §8.7 / §9.4."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-1","title":"4.1.1 — Listen: mark intro / verse / pre / chorus / bridge / outro","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.1 — Listen: mark intro / verse / pre / chorus / bridge / outro","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you already own: mark intro / verse / pre / chorus / bridge / outro.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Optional owned-catalog listens from the syllabus lock (<em>Umbrella</em>, <em>Chandelier</em>, <em>Blinding Lights</em>, <em>Somebody That I Used to Know</em>) — <strong>only if you already own them</strong>. Otherwise any radio song you own. Never rip."},{"type":"prose","html":"On paper or in clip names, mark: Intro / Verse / Pre (if any) / Chorus / Bridge / Outro. If a section fails the 0.4–0.8 tests, write <code>gap</code> — do not force a pre-chorus onto a song that does not have one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Six labels, or fewer with honest gaps."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-2","title":"4.1.2 — Why the chorus keeps its lyrics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.2 — Why the chorus keeps its lyrics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Why the chorus keeps its lyrics.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia / Walter Everett (via 0.5): the chorus usually <strong>retains the same lyrics</strong> every time its music appears; it is the higher-intensity section; it “nearly always prolongs the tonic and carries an <strong>unvaried</strong> poetic text.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Production translation: the <strong>chorus clip</strong> is the one you do not rewrite. Verse clips may change words (or a new hummed MIDI). If your “chorus” has new lyrics every time, you wrote another verse and called it a chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Instrumental pop: the <em>hook figure</em> stays. Treat the lead like an unvaried text (KB §8.6)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Which clip in your Set is not allowed to change its notes/words?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-3","title":"4.1.3 — Scene map for a radio-length idea","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.3 — Scene map for a radio-length idea","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Scene map for a radio-length idea.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Jason Blume / songwriting.net (0.8): a common hit form is verse–chorus–verse–chorus–bridge–chorus (<strong>ABABCB</strong>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Session map (names, not <code>scene 3</code>):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Intro</code> → <code>Verse</code> → <code>Pre</code> → <code>Chorus</code> → <code>Verse_2</code> → <code>Chorus</code> → <code>Bridge</code> → <code>Chorus_last</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You may omit <code>Pre</code> if you cannot hear a ramp (Summach: prechorus is not mandatory). Radio-length here means a <strong>sketch</strong> that could become 3-ish minutes — not a 90-minute DJ set. KB §9.7 first-song target was 2–2.5 minutes; this lab’s homework is 32–64 bars (3.1.11)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The scene list is written before you record a fourth layer."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-4","title":"4.1.4 — Verse clip vs chorus clip — density, not a new synth","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.4 — Verse clip vs chorus clip — density, not a new synth","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Density, not a new synth.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.4: at least <strong>two</strong> levers. This lab’s required pair is usually <strong>more/fewer instruments</strong> + one of: open hats, register lift, velocity (2.2.9 / 2.4.4)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate the verse scene. Add a clip or unmute a placeholder. Core Library only. If you opened Vital, you left the course."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Solo the clips that exist only in <code>Chorus</code>. There is at least one."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-5","title":"4.1.5 — Pre-chorus scene as a ramp","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.5 — Pre-chorus scene as a ramp","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Pre-chorus scene as a ramp.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach (MTO 2011, 0.6): the prechorus is often an expansion that <strong>aims</strong> at the chorus — teleology, not a third hook. KB energy example: Verse 55% → Pre <strong>70%</strong> → Chorus 90%."},{"type":"prose","html":"Session: <code>Pre</code> should feel unfinished. Typical ramps: hats enter, bass denser, a fill clip at the scene edge (2.4.5). If <code>Pre</code> already sounds like a chorus, you wrote <code>Chorus</code> twice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Launch Verse → Pre → Chorus. The middle row is a climb, not a destination."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-6","title":"4.1.6 — Bridge scene: contrast, then last chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.6 — Bridge scene: contrast, then last chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Bridge scene: contrast, then last chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Stolpe / Berklee (0.7): a bridge earns its keep with <strong>contrast–character–clarity</strong>, typically <strong>once</strong>, after chorus 2. KB §8.7: Bridge 40–70%, then Final Chorus 100%."},{"type":"prose","html":"Session: <code>Bridge</code> removes the hook clip or changes harmony/register. Then <code>Chorus_last</code> returns thicker (3.1.9). A bridge that is a quieter chorus with the same topline is a fake."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Bridge</code> fails the Everett “same lyric / thicker texture” chorus test on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-7","title":"4.1.7 — Optional post-chorus clip (Peres test)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.7 — Optional post-chorus clip (Peres test)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Optional post-chorus clip (Peres test).  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Asaf Peres (2018; 0.6): a post-chorus needs <strong>(1)</strong> a hooky unit after the chorus and <strong>(2)</strong> energy that does <strong>not</strong> drop to verse level."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot pass both tests, <strong>do not add the scene</strong>. ABABCB without a post-chorus is still correct (0.6 checkpoint)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you add <code>Post</code>: a short chant, a chopped hook, or a new ostinato — one extra clip, not a new song."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You either have a <code>Post</code> that passes Peres, or you wrote “no post-chorus.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-8","title":"4.1.8 — Intro from song material vs cold start","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.8 — Intro from song material vs cold start","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Intro from song material vs cold start.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.7: Intro establishes groove or a <strong>hook fragment</strong>. Two legal pop intros:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>From the song:</strong> chorus topline or drums, thinner.  ","<strong>Cold start:</strong> verse begins at bar 1. Wikipedia lists both."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"A 32-bar DJ intro is the <strong>other</strong> clock (0.9 / 5.1). Do not paste one into a radio lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Your <code>Intro</code> is either a fragment of a later clip, or you launched <code>Verse</code> first on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-9","title":"4.1.9 — Last chorus: one extra clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.9 — Last chorus: one extra clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Last chorus: one extra clip.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.3 cousin: copy the chorus block; vary the <strong>last</strong> chorus (extra harmony, wider sound, ad-libs). One extra clip — a doubled lead, an extra hat, a converted-hum answer phrase (1.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If last chorus = first chorus, the energy map’s 95–100% never happens."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Chorus_last</code> is not a duplicate-only row."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-10","title":"4.1.10 — MK4: verse → pre → chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.10 — MK4: verse → pre → chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On the Launchkey MK4 61: verse → pre → chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip mode. Scene launch. Hands only if the keyboard is present; mouse is legal (2.3.8). Feel the ramp you claimed in 3.1.5."},{"type":"prose","html":"MPE still off. Drum mode is not required unless a pad chop is a chorus extra."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Three launches, no mouse, if hardware is on the desk."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-11","title":"4.1.11 — 32–64 bar pop sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.11 — 32–64 bar pop sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> 32–64 bar pop sketch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Perform or Follow-Action a pass that lands between <strong>32 and 64 bars</strong>. That is a lab sketch, not a single. Example arithmetic (not a law): 8 Intro + 8 Verse + 4 Pre + 8 Chorus + 8 Verse_2 + 8 Chorus + 8 Bridge + 8 last ≈ 60 bars."},{"type":"prose","html":"Export a listen file into the Project folder. Name it <code>LAB_pop_sketch.wav</code>. This is <strong>not</strong> Gate C unless you later choose pop in 7.1 and finish it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The file exists and is not an 8-bar loop."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-1-12","title":"4.1.12 — Written section order of what you actually launched","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.12 — Written section order of what you actually launched","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Written section order of what you actually launched.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Write the <strong>real</strong> order, including mistakes and repeats. If you launched Chorus twice in a row, the page says so. Honesty is the lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 4.1 checkpoint:</strong> A stranger can follow your written order and hear verse vs chorus."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-1","title":"4.2.1 — Space as a section (rests are form)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.1 — Space as a section (rests are form)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Space as a section (rests are form).  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.5: melodies need <strong>air</strong>. In contemporary R&amp;B the air is often the arrangement: a bar of almost-nothing before the hook, a kick that waits, a chord that does not fill every beat."},{"type":"prose","html":"A rest is not an empty Session accident. It is a clip you <em>chose not to launch</em>, or an empty MIDI clip (2.2.8) that keeps the scene’s meaning."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One named scene is quieter because of <strong>missing</strong> events, not a lower fader."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-2","title":"4.2.2 — Verse / hook / break map","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.2 — Verse / hook / break map","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Verse / hook / break map.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"R&amp;B often uses <strong>verse / hook / break</strong> language alongside pop’s verse / chorus. The hook is the remembered line (cousin of Everett’s unvaried text). A <strong>break</strong> here is a pocket reset — not a jungle Amen science lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scene names: <code>Verse</code> · <code>Hook</code> · <code>Break</code> · <code>Hook_2</code>. You may still add <code>Bridge</code> if the song needs Stolpe contrast."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can say whether <code>Hook</code> equals pop <code>Chorus</code> in <em>this</em> sketch."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-3","title":"4.2.3 — Pocket: leave holes for a vocal that may not exist yet","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.3 — Pocket: leave holes for a vocal that may not exist yet","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Pocket: leave holes for a vocal that may not exist yet.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"If there is no singer, leave <strong>space on beats where a vocal would sit</strong>. Do not fill every eighth with keys. Hum-converted MIDI (1.5) can occupy the hole later."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is production, not mixing. The hole is a <strong>clip decision</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Mute the keys. Is there still a place a voice could enter?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-4","title":"4.2.4 — Sparse pad drums + keys","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.4 — Sparse pad drums + keys","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Sparse pad drums + keys.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"MK4 Drum mode: fewer pads, not a 16-pad roll. Stock kit. Keys: one chord clip, low velocity in the verse (2.2.9). Do not program 32nd hats (Drums course)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The verse drum clip would look lonely on a drum exam. That is the point."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-5","title":"4.2.5 — Hook returns with small variation, not a new song","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.5 — Hook returns with small variation, not a new song","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Hook returns with small variation, not a new song.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Same hook clip, second time: one change — a harmony note, an open hat, a doubled MIDI. Cole Mize’s rap-world cousin (4.1) says the hook is the <strong>busy</strong> section; R&amp;B can stay spare and still lift. Two levers still apply."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Hook_2</code> is 90% <code>Hook</code>, not a new topline."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-6","title":"4.2.6 — Ad-lib lane as a later scene","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.6 — Ad-lib lane as a later scene","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Ad-lib lane as a <em>later</em> scene.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia (0.7): <strong>ad lib</strong> in form language is usually a coda moment — the lead breaks the established lyric/melody. Distinct from “I improvised in Capture.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Add a scene <code>Adlib</code> or <code>Outro_adlib</code> <strong>after</strong> the last hook. One extra clip (a freer hummed MIDI, a thrown delay). Do not sprinkle ad-libs on every row or the form dissolves."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Ad-libs live in a <em>later</em> scene, not in Verse 1."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-7","title":"4.2.7 — MK4: hook vs verse velocity","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.7 — MK4: hook vs verse velocity","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On the Launchkey MK4 61: hook vs verse velocity.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Play the same keys part twice. Capture both. Keep soft for <code>Verse</code>, harder for <code>Hook</code>. Same notes. That is a §9.4 lever without a new patch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Two clips, same pitches, different velocity clouds."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l3-2-8","title":"4.2.8 — 32-bar sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.8 — 32-bar sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> 32-bar sketch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Thirty-two bars: e.g. 8 verse + 8 hook + 8 verse + 8 hook, or 8 / 8 / 8 break / 8 hook. Listen file <code>LAB_rnb_sketch.wav</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 4.2 checkpoint:</strong> A mute is an arrangement decision. You can point at the mute."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Module 3 (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Section 0.4–0.8 textbook (Wikipedia / Everett / Summach / Stolpe / Peres / Blume)  ","KB §8.5–8.7, §9.3–9.4  ","Syllabus listen-card lock (optional owned catalog only)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
